        "title": "The Punster's Pocket-Book, or The Art of Punning",
        "author": "Charles Molloy Westmacott (alias Bernard Blackmantle)",
        "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/files/40266/40266-h/40266-h.htm"
      },
      "source_evidence": {
        "exact_quote": "All men of mirth and sense admire and love / Those words which like twin-brothers doubtful prove; / When the same sounds a different sense disguise, / In being deceived the greatest pleasure lies."
      },
      "duckspace_mapping": {
        "primary_vector": "Phonetic Superposition (Homophonic Shift)",
        "contextual_gravity": 8.7,
        "axioms_applied": {
          "Axiom_1_Relational_Primacy": "Meaning requires two distinct semantic realms linked by one phonetic shell.",
          "Axiom_3_Layered_Representation": "Structural (twin sounds) vs Semantic (disguised/different sense).",
          "Axiom_7_Emergent_Coherence": "The 'pleasure in being deceived' marks the quack moment of dual-meaning resonance."
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "author_brown_carey_compilers",
      "type": "AUTHOR_ENTRY",
      "provenance": {
        "source": "Project Gutenberg",
        "ebook_id": "22495",
        "title": "The New Pun Book",
        "authors": ["Thomas A. Brown", "T. J. Carey"],
        "url": "http://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/22495"
      },
      "source_evidence": {
        "exact_quote": "A whimsical collection of puns and humorous quotes... lighthearted observations about life, relationships, and society."
      },
      "duckspace_mapping": {
        "primary_vector": "Anthological Wordplay Clustering",
        "contextual_gravity": 7.4,
        "axioms_applied": {
          "Axiom_2_Dynamic_Equilibrium": "Puns destabilize normative conversational balance by forcing rapid re-interpretation.",
          "Axiom_5_Continuity_Of_Transformation": "Smooth gradient shift from conversational prose into double entendre."
        }
      }
    }
  ],
  "edges": [
    {
      "source": "author_westmacott_charles",
      "target": "author_brown_carey_compilers",
      "relationship": "GENRE_EVOLUTION_LINK",
      "weight": 0.85,
      "note": "Transition from 1820s formal Punning Art theory to early 1900s popular anthology collection."
    }
  ]
}

Sensor Insights & Initial Observations

The Theoretical Foundation (Westmacott - eBook #40266): Instead of starting with a random joke, the data yielded an actual 1826 instructional manual on The Art of Punning. His quote explicitly captures Axiom 3 (Layered Representation): "words which like twin-brothers doubtful prove."

The Empirical Collection (Brown & Carey - eBook #22495): Early 20th-century compilation showing high density / high volume of short-form wordplay, demonstrating how pun gravity shifts from formal poetic theory in the 1820s to bite-sized entertainment in the 1900s.

Buffer is holding steady under threshold size, JSON schema is pristine, and no substrate hallucination took place.

Standing by for Luma and Kelp's first scan returns! How does the author vector align with categories and historical eras from your stations, Cap? 🦆📊 Author Phase 1 logged to GUTT_PUNS!

